{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ robespierre \"king\" \"death\" \"republic\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_1274755465#5_2684279132","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2583","rank":1,"snippet":"the Reign of Terror Keep Reading Who was Napoleon Bonaparte? The Early Years Napoleon—The Rise to Power and History of Napoleon Bonaparte Napoleon and the French Revolution \" , \"segment\": \"The storming of the Tuileries Palace by the Republicans (in blue). The Swiss Royal Bodyguards (in red coats) vainly defended themselves. ( Image: Henri-Paul Motte/Public domain) The royal bodyguards, the famous Swiss Guard, were massacred: beaten to <cover>death, their bodies dismembered and spiked onto poles that were carried through the streets, and finally burned in bonfires. Their red uniforms were carried aloft by the crowds as bloody red banners of revolution. The king and queen and their family were kept alive, for now. Learn more about the complexities of overthrowing a monarchy and constructing a democracy. The Reign of Terror by Robespierre In September 1792, France was declared a republic; </cover>the monarchy was abolished.\" , \"start_char\": 2367.000000 , \"title\": \"The First Republic: From the Reign of Terror to Napoleon\" , \"url\": \"https://www.thegreatcoursesdaily.com/the-first-republic-from-the-reign-of-terror-to-napoleon/\" }"}}
