{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_923016575#3_1795130740","relevance":3,"reason":"The dedicated Alpha History page on the trial and execution of Louis XVI directly reports Robespierre's argument that no trial was necessary because the people had already delivered their verdict on the king, supporting his rationale for execution.","learned":"Robespierre argued Louis XVI was already condemned by the people's revolutionary action on August 10, so the question was not ordinary trial procedure but revolutionary necessity."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2610","reason":"This query has found a dedicated trial-and-execution source with direct evidence about Robespierre's argument, so continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2610"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_923016575#4_1795132329","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2610","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_923016575#4_1795132329\" , \"end_char\": 3582.000000 , \"headings\": \"The trial and execution of Louis XVI The trial and <cover>execution of Louis XVI Contents Background Trial or no trial? The hearing begins Louis’ defence Guilt and sentencing The king condemned Responses \" , \"segment\": \"Two of the Convention’s deputies, Charles-François Morisson and Claude Fauchet, argued that the king could only be tried under the Constitution of 1791; there was no other law that applied to him. Jean-Marie Rouzet believed the king was answerable for his crimes but argued that placing him on trial was provocative, potentially disruptive and not in the national interest. In contrast, Maximilien Robespierre argued that no trial was necessary. </cover>The people had already delivered their verdict on the king, Robespierre argued, with their actions on August 10th. The hearing begins Raymond de Sèze, one of Louis XVI’s defence lawyers On December 3rd, the National Convention resolved that Louis could and would be put on trial. Controversially, the Convention itself would act as his judge and jury. "}}
